# Leaf Log — device HTTP ingress proxy
#
# The Leaf vario can't do TLS without stalling its background services, so it
# speaks PLAIN HTTP. Railway force-redirects HTTP->HTTPS on its normal web
# domains, so the device can't hit the app directly. This tiny Caddy service is
# the device's HTTP front door: plain HTTP in from the Leaf, forwarded to ONLY
# the device endpoints on the real app.
#
# Preferred deployment: run this as its own Railway service (root dir `proxy/`,
# built from the Dockerfile) with a **TCP Proxy** attached — that gives a raw-TCP
# (no-TLS, no-redirect) door at `<generated>.proxy.rlwy.net:<port>`. It can also
# run on any plain-HTTP host (VPS) via docker-compose. See README.md.
#
# Listens on $PORT (Railway injects it; defaults to :80 for a VPS) on ANY Host,
# since the TCP-proxy hostname is Railway-generated. Only the 3 device endpoints
# are exposed; everything else 404s. The website + session-authed pairing CLAIM
# stay HTTPS-only on the main domain and are not reachable here.
{
	auto_https off
	admin off
}

:{$PORT:80} {
	# Only the three device endpoints are exposed over HTTP.
	@device path /api/ingest /api/devices/pair/start /api/devices/pair/poll

	handle @device {
		# Upstream defaults to the public app over HTTPS (Caddy does the TLS, which
		# the Leaf can't). To go over Railway's private network instead, set
		# UPSTREAM=http://web.railway.internal:<app-port> and keep UPSTREAM_HOST as
		# the public hostname so the app sees the expected Host.
		reverse_proxy {$UPSTREAM:https://leaflog.norcalflight.com} {
			header_up Host {$UPSTREAM_HOST:leaflog.norcalflight.com}
			header_up X-Forwarded-Proto http
		}
	}

	# Liveness probe for the device / monitoring.
	handle /healthz {
		respond "ok" 200
	}

	# Nothing else is available over the HTTP door.
	handle {
		respond "Not found" 404
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
